The purpose of this article is to show how `mvgam` can be used to fit
and interrogate multi-season occupancy models for species that are
detected imperfectly.

## Occupancy models

An occupancy model is a standard part of the ecological modeller's
toolkit for making inferences about where a species occurs when our
surveys can miss it (MacKenzie et al.
[2002](https://doi.org/10.1890/0012-9658(2002)083[2248:ESORWD]2.0.CO;2)).
Briefly, assume $y_{s,t,j}$ records whether the species was detected
(1) or not (0) at site $s$ on replicate visit $j$ in season $t$. If
the visits within a season are close enough together to satisfy the
assumption that each site's status remained closed (i.e. the species
did not colonise or abandon the site between visits), we can account
for the fact that a non-detection does not always mean the species is
absent. This is done by assuming the detections are Bernoulli random
variables that depend on the true "latent" occupancy status $z$ and a
detection probability $p$:

\begin{align*}
y_{s,t,j} & \sim \text{Bernoulli}(z_{s,t} \, p_{s,t,j}) \\
z_{s,t} & \sim \text{Bernoulli}(\psi_{s,t})
\end{align*}

Using a set of linear predictors, we can estimate effects of
covariates on the probability of occupancy $\psi$ (with a logit link)
and, jointly, effects of possibly different covariates on detection
probability $p$ (also with a logit link). In the example below,
occupancy depends on a smooth function of forest cover and on a
latent AR(1) process $\eta_t$ that all sites share. Detection depends
on how much rain fell during each visit:

\begin{align*}
\text{logit}(\psi_{s,t}) & = \alpha + f(\text{forest}_s) + \eta_t \\
\eta_t & \sim \text{Normal}(\phi \, \eta_{t-1}, \sigma) \\
\text{logit}(p_{s,t,j}) & = \gamma + \delta \, \text{rain}_{s,t,j}
\end{align*}

`mvgam` can handle this type of model because it is designed to
propagate unobserved temporal processes that evolve independently of
the observation process. An occupancy model fits this State-Space
format well, as it can be thought of as a State-Space model whose
latent state is the binary "true" occupancy status of each site. This
is very convenient. We can incorporate any of the package's diverse
effect types (i.e. multidimensional splines, time-varying effects,
random effects etc...) into either linear predictor, while the latent
process links the seasons together and lets us forecast occupancy into
seasons that have not yet been surveyed. All that is required is a
marginalization trick that sums the likelihood over both possible
values of $z$, leaving `Stan`'s samplers to deal only with continuous
parameters (see more about this in the `Stan` User's Guide chapter on
[latent discrete parameters](https://mc-stan.org/docs/stan-users-guide/latent-discrete.html)).

## Simulating multi-season detection data

First we will simulate detection / non-detection data for a single
species surveyed at 40 sites over ten seasons, with four replicate
visits per season. Occupancy probability increases with forest cover
but levels off at the most forested sites. A regional AR(1) process
causes occupancy at all sites to rise and fall together from one
season to the next, as we might expect if the species responds to
good and bad years across the region. Detection probability declines
with the amount of rain recorded on each visit:

```{r simulate}
set.seed(42)
n_site <- 40
n_season <- 10
n_visit <- 4

# Forest cover of each site, with mean 0 and SD 1
forest <- round(as.numeric(scale(runif(n_site))), 2)

# Regional AR(1) deviations in logit occupancy, shared by every site
z <- numeric(n_season)
z[1] <- rnorm(1, 0, 0.6)
for (t in 2:n_season) z[t] <- 0.7 * z[t - 1] + rnorm(1, 0, 0.45)

# Occupancy rises with forest cover and levels off
f_forest <- function(x) 1.6 * tanh(1.2 * x)
psi <- plogis(outer(f_forest(forest), z, "+") - 0.2)
occupied <- matrix(rbinom(length(psi), 1, psi), n_site, n_season)

# One row per visit, with rain recorded on each
dat <- expand.grid(visit = seq_len(n_visit), time = seq_len(n_season),
                   site = seq_len(n_site))
dat$series <- factor("species1")
dat$site <- factor(dat$site)
dat$forest <- forest[as.integer(dat$site)]
dat$rain <- round(runif(nrow(dat)), 2)
dat$occupied <- occupied[cbind(as.integer(dat$site), dat$time)]

# An occupied site is detected with probability p, which falls with
# rain. An empty site is never detected.
p <- plogis(0.5 - 2 * dat$rain)
dat$y <- rbinom(nrow(dat), 1, dat$occupied * p)
```

Preview the dataset to get an idea of how it is structured:

```{r preview}
head(dat)
```

Each row is one visit. `series` identifies the species, `site` the
site and `time` the season. The `occupied` column holds the true
simulated occupancy status, which we will use to check our estimates
(the model never sees it). This long format differs from the
multidimensional detection arrays that are commonly used to fit
occupancy models in other software, but `pivot_detection_array()` can
convert those arrays for you. Next we split the data into training
and testing sets, holding out the last two seasons to evaluate
forecasts:

```{r split}
train <- subset(dat, time <= 8)
test <- subset(dat, time > 8)
```

It is always worth plotting the detection histories before fitting
anything. Here we sum the detections at each site in each training
season and order the sites by their forest cover:

```{r histories, fig.asp = 0.75, fig.cap = "Number of detections from four visits at each site in each training season. Sites are ordered by forest cover, with the most forested sites at the top."}
histories <- aggregate(y ~ site + time + forest, data = train, FUN = sum)
histories$site <- reorder(histories$site, histories$forest)
ggplot(histories, aes(x = time, y = site, fill = factor(y))) +
  geom_tile(colour = "white") +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c("grey92", "#DCBCBC", "#C79999", "#A25050", "#7C0000"),
    name = "Detections"
  ) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 1:8, expand = c(0, 0)) +
  labs(x = "Season", y = "Site (ordered by forest cover)") +
  theme(axis.text.y = element_blank(), axis.ticks.y = element_blank())
```

Detections are concentrated at the more forested sites. Some seasons
(such as season 5) also produced detections at many more sites than
others. Notice how rarely an occupied site produces detections on all
four visits. Some of the grey cells will be sites where the species
was present but missed on every visit. We can see the forest cover
relationship more directly by plotting the proportion of seasons in
which each site produced at least one detection:

```{r naive-forest, fig.cap = "Proportion of training seasons with at least one detection at each site, against standardised forest cover."}
seen <- aggregate(y ~ site + forest + time, data = train, FUN = max)
by_site <- aggregate(y ~ site + forest, data = seen, FUN = mean)
ggplot(by_site, aes(x = forest, y = y)) +
  geom_point(size = 2, alpha = 0.7) +
  labs(x = "Forest cover (standardised)",
       y = "Proportion of seasons with a detection")
```

This relationship looks nonlinear, with few detections at the least
forested sites and a plateau at the most forested ones. But these raw
proportions mix occupancy with detection. Imperfect detection pulls
them below the true occupancy probabilities. Separating these two
processes is exactly what the occupancy model is for.

## Modelling with the `occ()` family

Now we are ready to fit a model using `mvgam()`. Occupancy and
detection each get their own formula inside `bf()`. The first formula
sets up the linear predictor for occupancy on the logit scale, while
the second (`p ~`) sets up the linear predictor for detection
probability. `trend_formula = ~ AR()` adds the regional AR(1) process
to logit occupancy. Setting `multi_season = TRUE` in `occ()` tells
`mvgam` that each combination of site and season is its own closure
unit (i.e. occupancy can change between seasons but not between visits
within a season):

```{r fit}
mod <- mvgam(
  bf(y ~ s(forest, k = 5), p ~ rain),
  trend_formula = ~ AR(),
  data = train,
  newdata = test,
  family = occ(multi_season = TRUE),
  chains = 4, seed = 42, silent = 2
)
```

```{r rain-numbers, include = FALSE}
rain <- as.numeric(as_draws_matrix(mod, variable = "b_p_rain"))
fe_rain <- sprintf("%.2f [%.2f, %.2f]", mean(rain),
                   quantile(rain, 0.025), quantile(rain, 0.975))
```

The posterior summary separates parameters that belong to the
observation model from those that belong to the latent AR(1) process:

```{r summary}
summary(mod)
```

The estimated effect of rain on detection (`p_rain`) is `r fe_rain` on
the logit scale, which is close to the true simulated value of $-2$.

## Estimating the proportion of sites occupied

A common goal in occupancy modelling is to estimate how many sites the
species occupies, which will usually be more than the number of
sites where it happened to be detected (often called naive
occupancy). `hindcast(type = "latent_state")` returns posterior
probabilities that each site was occupied in each season, conditional
on the detections observed there. Its `plot()` method averages these
probabilities over sites to give the expected proportion of sites
occupied in each season. Here we add the true proportions (black
points) and naive occupancy (open circles) for comparison:

```{r occupancy-plot, fig.cap = "Expected proportion of sites occupied in each training season, with 50% and 95% credible intervals. Black points show the true proportion. Open circles show naive occupancy, the proportion of sites with at least one detection."}
hc <- hindcast(mod, type = "latent_state")
units <- unique(train[, c("site", "time", "forest", "occupied")])
occ_true <- aggregate(occupied ~ time, data = units, FUN = mean)
detected <- aggregate(y ~ site + time, data = train, FUN = max)
occ_naive <- aggregate(y ~ time, data = detected, FUN = mean)
plot(hc) +
  geom_point(data = occ_naive, aes(x = time, y = y), shape = 21,
             fill = "white", colour = "grey30", size = 1.6) +
  geom_point(data = occ_true, aes(x = time, y = occupied), size = 1.6) +
  labs(y = "Proportion of sites occupied")
```

```{r occupancy-numbers, include = FALSE}
n_below <- sum(occ_naive$y < occ_true$occupied)
state <- merge(as.data.frame(hc), detected, by = c("site", "time"))
state <- merge(state, units, by = c("site", "time"))
missed <- subset(state, y == 0)
n_missed <- sum(missed$occupied)
by_status <- function(x) {
  sprintf("%.2f", tapply(x, factor(missed$occupied, 1:0), mean))
}
psi_missed <- by_status(missed$median)
forest_missed <- by_status(missed$forest)
train$p <- predict(mod, type = "detection")[, "Estimate"]
miss <- aggregate(cbind(q = 1 - p) ~ site + time, data = train, FUN = prod)
p_miss <- sprintf("%.2f", mean(miss$q))
prop <- sapply(1:8, function(t) {
  rowMeans(hc$draws[, hc$unit$time == t])
})
band <- apply(prop, 2, quantile, c(0.025, 0.975))
n_outside <- sum(occ_true$occupied < band[1, ] |
                   occ_true$occupied > band[2, ])
```

Naive occupancy underestimates the true proportion of occupied sites
in `r n_below` of the 8 training seasons. This happens because the
species is fairly easy to miss: given the rain recorded on its
visits, an occupied site has an average probability of `r p_miss` of
going undetected on all four visits. The model accounts for these
missed detections. Its estimates track the true proportions from
season to season. The intervals show uncertainty in the expected
proportion of sites occupied. Because the true proportion in any one
season also depends on which of the undetected sites happen to be
occupied, `r n_outside` of the 8 true values fall outside the 95%
intervals.

Since a single detection confirms that a site is occupied, all of the
uncertainty in these estimates comes from sites where the species was
never detected. In the training data there are `r nrow(missed)`
combinations of site and season with no detections. The species was
present in `r n_missed` of them. `as.data.frame(hc)` returns one row
per site and season, which makes it easy to inspect these directly.
The model's estimated occupancy probability averages `r psi_missed[1]`
at the sites that were occupied but missed, compared to
`r psi_missed[2]` at the sites that were empty. The forest cover
effect explains this difference, as the occupied but missed sites are
more heavily forested (average standardised forest cover of
`r forest_missed[1]` vs `r forest_missed[2]`).

## Habitat and detection effects

`conditional_effects()` supports the occupancy prediction types. Using
`type = "latent_state"` will plot the estimated effect of forest cover
on the probability of occupancy. Because the result is a `ggplot`
object, we can easily add the true simulated function as a dashed
line:

```{r forest-effect, fig.cap = "Occupancy probability against standardised forest cover, with its 95% credible interval. The dashed line is the true simulated function."}
forest_grid <- data.frame(forest = seq(min(forest), max(forest),
                                       length.out = 100))
forest_grid$psi <- plogis(f_forest(forest_grid$forest) - 0.2)
plot(conditional_effects(mod, effects = "forest", type = "latent_state"),
     plot = FALSE)[[1]] +
  geom_line(data = forest_grid, aes(x = forest, y = psi), linetype = 2)
```

The smooth has done a nice job of capturing the saturating effect of
forest cover, which increases occupancy steeply across the middle of
the forest gradient before levelling off at the most forested sites.
Using `type = "detection"` instead will plot the estimated effect of
rain on detection probability:

```{r rain-effect, out.width = "70%", fig.cap = "Detection probability against the rain index, with its 95% credible interval."}
plot(conditional_effects(mod, effects = "rain", type = "detection"),
     ask = FALSE)
```

## Forecasting occupancy

Because the latent AR(1) process links the seasons together, we can
forecast occupancy into the two seasons we held out.
`forecast(type = "latent_state")` propagates the AR(1) process forward,
combines it with each site's forest cover and averages over sites to
give the expected proportion of sites occupied in each future season:

```{r forecast, fig.cap = "Expected proportion of sites occupied over the training seasons and the two held-out seasons. Points show the true proportion."}
fc <- forecast(mod, type = "latent_state")
occ_true_all <- aggregate(
  occupied ~ time, data = unique(dat[, c("site", "time", "occupied")]),
  FUN = mean
)
plot(fc) +
  geom_point(data = occ_true_all, aes(x = time, y = occupied),
             size = 1.6) +
  labs(y = "Proportion of sites occupied")
```

```{r forecast-numbers, include = FALSE}
fc_q <- apply(fc$forecasts[[1]], 2, quantile, c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975))
fc_text <- sprintf("%.2f [%.2f, %.2f]", fc_q[2, ], fc_q[1, ], fc_q[3, ])
```

The forecast proportions of sites occupied are `r fc_text[1]` and
`r fc_text[2]` for the two held-out seasons, compared to true
proportions of `r sprintf("%.2f", occ_true_all$occupied[9])` and
`r sprintf("%.2f", occ_true_all$occupied[10])`. The forecast intervals
are much wider than the hindcast intervals because no detections from
these seasons enter the model. If we instead want forecasts of the
detections themselves, the default `forecast(mod)` will produce these
for each visit in the held-out seasons. `score()` can then evaluate
them against the observed detections.

## Checking the fit

`pp_check(type = "fit_stat")` computes the chi-squared statistic of
MacKenzie and Bailey
([2004](https://doi.org/10.1198/108571104X3361)), which compares the
total detections at each site in each season against their
expectations. The same statistic is then computed on detections
simulated from the posterior. A Bayesian p-value near 0 would mean the
simulated detections rarely reach the discrepancy of the observed
ones:

```{r fit-stat}
fit_stat <- pp_check(mod, type = "fit_stat")
fit_stat
```

```{r fit-stat-numbers, include = FALSE}
fe_fit <- if (fit_stat$bayes_p > 0.05) {
  "gives no sign of lack of fit"
} else {
  "suggests the model misses some variation among sites"
}
```

The value here, `r sprintf("%.2f", fit_stat$bayes_p)`, `r fe_fit`. We
can also inspect randomised quantile residuals, which `mvgam` computes
for each site in each season from its total detections:

```{r residuals, out.width = "70%", fig.cap = "Normal Q-Q plot of the randomised quantile residuals, one for each site in each season."}
plot(mod, type = "residuals")
```

The residuals fall close to the line, giving us confidence that the
model is capturing the main sources of variation in these detection
histories.

## Writing up the model

`methods_md()` writes out the fitted model as Markdown with LaTeX
equations, including the data dimensions, every linear predictor, the
priors and the call that produced it. This makes it easy to drop a
complete model description into a manuscript or supplement:

```{r methods, results = "asis"}
methods_md(mod, heading_level = 3)
```

`how_to_cite()` complements this with a methods paragraph and the
references for each component of the model.

## Extending the model

Because both formulas are passed to `brms`, the occupancy and detection
linear predictors can include any of the effects that `brms` and
`mgcv` support. For example, we might give each observer their own
detection intercept, add a Gaussian process over site coordinates to
capture spatial variation in occupancy and place a regularizing prior
on the detection coefficients. Priors for the detection predictor are
identified with `dpar = p`:

```{r extend, eval = FALSE}
mod_ext <- mvgam(
  bf(y ~ s(forest, k = 5) + gp(x, y_coord, k = 10, c = 5 / 4),
     p ~ rain + (1 | observer)),
  trend_formula = ~ AR(),
  data = train,
  family = occ(multi_season = TRUE),
  prior = prior(normal(0, 1), class = b, dpar = p)
)
```

Here `x` and `y_coord` would hold the coordinates of each site and
`observer` would identify who carried out each visit. `get_prior()`
lists every parameter whose prior can be changed in this way.

## Further reading

Each function used here has a help page with more details, such as
`?occ` or `?hindcast.mvgam`. The [N-mixture models article](nmix.html) shows how to
estimate abundance from repeated counts using the same data layout,
while the [Joint species distribution models article](jsdgam.html)
shows how to share latent factors among many species. The following
papers offer useful material about occupancy models:

MacKenzie DI and Bailey LL (2004). Assessing the fit of site-occupancy
models. *Journal of Agricultural, Biological, and Environmental
Statistics* 9(3), 300-318.
[doi:10.1198/108571104X3361](https://doi.org/10.1198/108571104X3361)

MacKenzie DI, Nichols JD, Lachman GB, Droege S, Royle JA and
Langtimm CA (2002). Estimating site occupancy rates when detection
probabilities are less than one. *Ecology* 83(8), 2248-2255.
[doi:10.1890/0012-9658(2002)083[2248:ESORWD]2.0.CO;2](https://doi.org/10.1890/0012-9658(2002)083%5B2248:ESORWD%5D2.0.CO;2)
